Undefined constructor error in a double bracket collection initializer 

In the following code the `getEntriesNotWorking` method reports a compile-time error: 


    public class DoubleBracketInitializerTest {
    class Container {}
    class Entry {
    Container container;
    public Entry(Container container) {
    this.container = container;
    }
    }
    class DetailedContainer extends Container {
    List getEntriesNotWorking() {
    return new ArrayList() {{
    add(new Entry(this)); // compile-time error mentioned below
    }};
    }
    List getEntriesWorking() {
    return Arrays.asList(new Entry(this));
    }
    }
    }


Error:

> The constructor DoubleBracketInitializerTest.Entry(new ArrayList(){}) is undefined

While the `getEntriesWorking()` method is compiling correctly. The constructor is clearly there since a `DetailedContailer` is a subclass of `Contailer`. 

What is the difference between those two methods, that makes the code invalid? How can I make the double bracket initializer work for this class hierarchy?

The double-braces initialization:


    return new ArrayList() {{
    add(new Entry(this)); // compile-time error mentioned below
    }};


creates an anonymous inner class, which is a subclass of `ArrayList` in this case. So, the `this` reference refers to an instance of the subclass of `ArrayList` and not the `DetailedContainer` . To get the `this` reference of `DetailedContainer` class current instance, use `DetailedContainer.this`


    return new ArrayList() {{
    add(new Entry(DetailedContainer.this)); 
    }};


Of course, if you don't want a fixed sized list, you can directly use:


    return new ArrayList(Arrays.asList(new Entry(this)));


instead of anonymous class. That is lot easier to understand.

* * *

See also:

  * Accessing outer class members from within an inner class extending the outer class itself \- for detailed explanation.

Immutable collections in Java 

I have recently started looking at GUAVA's collections, namely `ImmutableList` and that seems rather cumbersome (with the builder instance etc.) Is there a library that would mimic a more "natural" way of how the collections should behave (scala's http://www.scala-lang.org/api/current/index.html#scala.collection.immutable.List is one example). I would like something that allows addition/removal etc. but preserving immutability and, perhaps for the purpose of performance, reuses parts of old list.

If I understand you correctly, you want an immutable list that has convenient add/remove methods that return new list instances that reuse as much of the original list structure as possible. You could do something like this:


    public abstract class ImmutableList implements Iterable {
    /**
    * Adds an element to the head of the list, returning the new list.
    *
    * @param o The element to be added to the list.
    * @return The list consisting of the element o followed by
    * this list.
    */
    public final ImmutableList add(final T o) {
    return new Node<>(o, this);
    }
    /**
    * Removes the element o resulting in a new list which
    * is returned to the caller.
    *
    * @param o The object to be removed from the list.
    * @return A list consisting of this list with object o removed.
    */
    public abstract ImmutableList remove(final T o);
    public abstract boolean isEmpty();
    public abstract int size();
    public abstract boolean contains(final T o);
    private ImmutableList() {}
    /**
    * Returns a "standard" enumeration over the elements of the list.
    */
    public Iterator iterator() {
    return new NodeIterator<>(this);
    }
    /**
    * The empty list. Variables of type ImmutableList should be
    * initialised to this value to create new empty lists.
    */
    private static final ImmutableList EMPTY = new ImmutableList() {
    @Override
    public ImmutableList remove(final Object o) {
    return this;
    }
    @Override
    public boolean isEmpty() {
    return true;
    }
    @Override
    public int size() {
    return 0;
    }
    @Override
    public boolean contains(final Object o) {
    return false;
    }
    };
    @SuppressWarnings("unchecked")
    public static  ImmutableList empty() {
    return (ImmutableList)EMPTY;
    }
    public static  ImmutableList create(final T head) {
    return new Node<>(head, ImmutableList.empty());
    }
    static class Node extends ImmutableList {
    private final int _size;
    private Node(final T element, final ImmutableList next) {
    _element = element;
    _next = ArgumentHelper.verifyNotNull(next, "next");
    _size = next.size() + 1;
    }
    public ImmutableList remove(final T old) {
    if (_element == old) {
    return _next;
    }
    else {
    final ImmutableList n = _next.remove(old);
    if (n == _next) {
    return this;
    }
    else {
    return new Node<>(_element, n);
    }
    }
    }
    @Override
    public boolean isEmpty() {
    return false;
    }
    @Override
    public int size() {
    return _size;
    }
    @Override
    public boolean contains(final T o) {
    return Objects.equals(_element, o) || _next.contains(o);
    }
    private final T _element;
    private final ImmutableList _next;
    }
    private class NodeIterator implements Iterator {
    private ImmutableList _current;
    private NodeIterator(final ImmutableList head) {
    _current = ArgumentHelper.verifyNotNull(head, "head");
    }
    public boolean hasNext() {
    return !_current.isEmpty();
    }
    public T next() {
    final T result = ((Node)_current)._element;
    _current = ((Node)_current)._next;
    return result;
    }
    public void remove() {
    throw new UnsupportedOperationException();
    }
    }
    }


For this implementation, a new list is build by adding item(s) to `ImmutableList.empty()`.

Note that this is not a particularly wonderful implementation; new elements are appended to the beginning of the list, as opposed to the end. But perhaps this will give you an idea of where to start.

