Merge multiple lists based on List priority I have a few lists, and each list has a different priority. When I merge these lists I expect to see the "most common" items first (those that appear in all lists), followed by less common items ordered by the priority of the list they came from. Here is an example use case of three lists with overlapping content.


    lStr lStr2 lStr3
    111 111
    112 112
    113 113 113
    114
    115
    118
    119 119
    120


The Merged list is expected to be like this:


    113 -- this should come on top as it is common in all 3 
    112 -- this should come next as it is common to lStr and lStr2
    111 -- this should come next as it is common to lStr and lStr3
    114 -- this is not common to any but has priority 1
    115 -- this is not common to any but has priority 1
    119 -- this is common with lstr3 and lstr2
    118 -- this is not common but has priority 2
    120 -- this is not common but has priority 3


The sample code below matches this use case, building the three input lists. How do I merge these lists as described, respecting list priority and repetition? Note: Please keep performance issues in mind, also list size, and number of lists may vary. 


    import java.util.ArrayList;
    import java.util.List;
    public class ListMerge {
    public static void main(String args[]){
    List lStr = new ArrayList(); // has priority 1
    List lStr2 = new ArrayList(); // has priority 2
    List lStr3 = new ArrayList(); // has priority 3
    // find another use case whith equal priority
    List lStr4 = new ArrayList(); // has priority 2 // has equal priority to lStr2
    List lStr5 = new ArrayList(); // has priority 1 // has equal priority to lStr
    lStr.add("111");
    lStr.add("112");
    lStr.add("113");// common
    lStr.add("114");
    lStr.add("115");
    System.out.println(lStr);
    lStr2.add("112");
    lStr2.add("113"); // common
    lStr2.add("118");
    lStr2.add("119");
    System.out.println(lStr2); 
    lStr3.add("113");// common
    lStr3.add("119");// common to lsr2 
    lStr3.add("111");// common to lsr1
    lStr3.add("120");// new 
    // when the merge happens the result list should like like the following 
    // use case 1 with different priorities 
    // sorted data should look similar to follow
    /*
    113 -- this should come on top as it is common in all 3 
    112 -- this should come next as it is common to lStr and lStr2
    111 -- this should come next as it is common to lStr and lStr3
    114 -- this is not common to any but has priority 1
    115 -- this is not common to any but has priority 1
    119 -- this is common with lstr3 and lstr2
    118 -- this has priority than any lstr2
    120 -- this has the lowest priority
    */
    // use case 2 with some cases with similar priorities 
    }
    }


Not entirely clear how the rules for combining priority are, but I suggest you just try to add up all the priorities the items have in the different lists and sort them by that combined priority.


    public static  List priorize(Map, Integer> listsToPriority) {
    Map totalPriority = new HashMap();
    List allElements = new ArrayList<>();
    for (List list : listsToPriority.keySet()) {
    int priority = listsToPriority.get(list);
    for (T x : list) {
    if (totalPriority.containsKey(x)) {
    totalPriority.put(x, totalPriority.get(x) + priority);
    } else {
    totalPriority.put(x, priority);
    allElements.add(x);
    }
    }
    }
    // sort by total priority in reverse order
    allElements.sort(new Comparator () {
    public int compare(T x, T y) {
    return totalPriority.get(y).compareTo(totalPriority.get(x)); 
    }
    });
    return allElements;
    }


Usage:


    Map, Integer> priorizedLists = new HashMap<>();
    priorizedLists.put(lStr, 3); // 3 == high priority
    priorizedLists.put(lStr2, 2);
    priorizedLists.put(lStr3, 1); // 1 == low priority
    priorizedLists.put(lStr4, 2);
    priorizedLists.put(lStr5, 3);
    List priorized = priorize(priorizedLists);
    System.out.println(priorized);


Output:


    [113, 112, 111, 119, 114, 115, 118, 120]


Some notes:

  * For this approach, items with high priority should actually have a high priority number, i.e. what has priority 1 in your example, has priority 3 in mine, but this should be easy to translate.
  * The output does not exactly match your output, but as I said, the rules for combining priorities are not 100% clear. You can replace the `+` with `*` to compute the product of all the priorities; this will in fact get the result that you want, but I do not know whether this makes sense in your setting.

