Although you get a `ConcurrentModificationException` your problem is not caused by multi-threading at all. In fact this is the problematic code:


    for (String job : jobs) {
    System.out.println("Job completed: " + job);
    jobs.remove(job);
    Thread.sleep(2000);
    }


The `for` loop will use an `Iterator` over jobs and at the same time you directly (structurally) modify `jobs` (i.e. not via the `Iterator`). This means that the `Iterator` is no longer well-defined and it throws this exception to tell you about it. If you need to remove elements from a collection while you iterate over it, you'll need to make the `Iterator` explicit:


    Iterator jobIterator = job.iterator();
    while (jobIterator.hasNext()) {
    String job = jobIterator.next();
    // ... stuff ...
    jobIterator.remove(); // remove the last object that was returned by next()
    }

