Can an iterator prevent its LinkedList from being garbage collected? In Java, is it safe to create a LinkedList in a method and return its iterator like so:


    public Iterator iterator()
    {
    LinkedList list = new LinkedList<>();
    list.add(1);
    return list.iterator();
    }


Is there any chance the list will be garbage-collected after the method returns? The code is safe, in the sense that as long as the Iterator is reachable from any active thread it cannot be finalized, and it will continue to work. In theory, if the underlying List is unmodifiable, its `iterator()` implementation could make its own copy of the list data, and not keep any pointer to the List. In that case, the List could be garbage collected. The Iterator would continue to work. In practice, all the `iterator()` implementations I've seen keep a reference to the Collection, either explicitly or by being an inner class. The Collection remains reachable as long as the Iterator is reachable.

