How to remove all duplicated strings from a Java List? For a given list, say `[ "a", "a", "b", "c", "c" ]` I need `[ "b" ]` (only non duplicated elements) as output. Note that this is different from using the `Set` interface for the job... I wrote the following code to do this in Java:


    void unique(List list) {
    Collections.sort(list);
    List dup = new ArrayList<>();
    int i = 0, j = 0;
    for (String e : list) {
    i = list.indexOf(e);
    j = list.lastIndexOf(e);
    if (i != j && !dup.contains(e)) {
    dup.add(e);
    }
    }
    list.removeAll(dup);
    }


It works... but for a list of size 85320, ends after several minutes! You best performance is with set:


     String[] xs = { "a", "a", "b", "c", "c" };
    Set singles = new TreeSet<>();
    Set multiples = new TreeSet<>();
    for (String x : xs) {
    if(!multiples.contains(x)){
    if(singles.contains(x)){
    singles.remove(x);
    multiples.add(x);
    }else{
    singles.add(x);
    }
    }
    }


It's a single pass and insert , remove and contains are log(n).

